  [2m▎[0m [3m"Time and tide wait for no one."[0m                            
                                                                
  That old proverb is practical advice. The water moves faster  
  than it appears, especially across flat sand, so build in a   
  safety margin.                                                
                                                                
  [1mWorking Out Your Window[0m                                       
                                                                
  Tides change gradually, not in a straight line. The water     
  moves slowly just after a high or low, then speeds up through
  the middle of the cycle. A handy shortcut is the "rule of   
  twelfths," which says the tide moves roughly one twelfth of
  its range in the first hour, two in the second, three in the  
  third, three in the fourth, two in the fifth, and one in the
  last.                                                         
                                                              
  You can also estimate the time between two tide events with a 
  few lines of code:                                            
                                                           
  [34mconst[39m low = [34mnew[39m [34mDate[39m([31m"2026-10-06T07:42:00"[39m);                 
  [34mconst[39m high = [34mnew[39m [34mDate[39m([31m"2026-10-06T13:55:00"[39m);               
  [34mconst[39m hours = (high - low) / ([32m1000[39m * [32m60[39m * [32m60[39m);              
  console.[33mlog[39m([31m`Rising for [39m${hours.toFixed([32m1[31m)}[39m hours`);        
  console.[33mlog[39m([31m`Roughly [39m${(hours / [32m6[31m).toFixed([32m2[31m)}[39m hours per
                                                              
